{
  "step_by_step_thinking": "The patient is an 87-year-old male with admission weight 48 kg, height 163 cm, and serum creatinine 1.4 mg/dL. First calculate BMI: 48 / (1.63^2) = 18.1 kg/m^2, which is underweight. Per the instructions, if underweight, use actual body weight as the adjusted body weight. Use the Cockcroft-Gault equation for males: CrCl = ((140 - age) * weight) / (72 * serum creatinine). Substituting values: ((140 - 87) * 48) / (72 * 1.4) = (53 * 48) / 100.8 = 2544 / 100.8 = 25.2 mL/min.",
  "answer": "25.2 mL/min"
}

{
  "step_by_step_thinking": "Patient is an 83-year-old male with admission creatinine 1.4 mg/dL, weight 67.2 kg, and height 173 cm. BMI = 67.2 / (1.73^2) ≈ 22.45, which is normal. For normal BMI, use the minimum of ideal body weight and actual weight. Male ideal body weight by Devine formula: 50 kg + 2.3 kg per inch over 5 ft. Height 173 cm ≈ 68.1 inches, so IBW ≈ 50 + 2.3*(8.1) ≈ 68.65 kg. Minimum of IBW and actual weight is 67.2 kg. Cockcroft-Gault for males: CrCl = (140 - age) * weight / (72 * SCr) = (140 - 83) * 67.2 / (72 * 1.4) = 57 * 67.2 / 100.8 = 3830.4 / 100.8 = 38.0 mL/min.",
  "answer": "38 mL/min"
}

{
  "step_by_step_thinking": "The patient is a 42-year-old female with height 160 cm (about 63 in), weight 105 kg, BMI 42 kg/m2, and serum creatinine 0.8 mg/dL. Because her BMI indicates obesity, use adjusted body weight. Ideal body weight for women = 45.5 kg + 2.3 kg per inch over 5 ft = 45.5 + 2.3*(63-60) = 52.4 kg. Adjusted body weight = IBW + 0.4*(actual weight - IBW) = 52.4 + 0.4*(105 - 52.4) = 73.44 kg. Cockcroft-Gault equation: CrCl = [(140 - age) * weight] / (72 * serum creatinine) * 0.85 for females. CrCl = [(140 - 42) * 73.44] / (72 * 0.8) * 0.85 = 106.2 mL/min.",
  "answer": "106.2 mL/min"
}

{
  "step_by_step_thinking": "1) Identify admission values: age = 45 years, sex = female, height = 156 cm, weight = 58 kg, serum creatinine = 0.7 mg/dL. 2) Calculate BMI: 58 / (1.56^2) = 23.8 kg/m^2, which is in the normal range. 3) For normal BMI, use the minimum of ideal body weight and actual weight. Ideal body weight for a woman using the Devine formula: 45.5 kg + 2.3 kg per inch over 5 ft. Height 156 cm = 61.42 in, which is 1.42 in over 60 in. IBW = 45.5 + 2.3 * 1.42 = 48.76 kg. Minimum of IBW (48.76 kg) and actual weight (58 kg) is 48.76 kg. 4) Apply Cockcroft-Gault: CrCl = [(140 - age) * weight * 0.85] / (72 * serum creatinine). CrCl = [(140 - 45) * 48.76 * 0.85] / (72 * 0.7) = 78.1 mL/min.",
  "answer": "78.1 mL/min"
}

{
  "step_by_step_thinking": "The patient is a 41-year-old male with height 167 cm, weight 52.5 kg, and admission serum creatinine 0.86 mg/dL. First calculate BMI: 52.5 / (1.67^2) = 18.8 kg/m^2, which is in the normal range. For a normal BMI, the adjusted body weight is the minimum of ideal body weight and actual body weight. Using the Devine equation for men, ideal body weight = 50 kg + 2.3 kg per inch over 5 ft. Height 167 cm is about 65.75 in, so IBW = 50 + 2.3 * (65.75 - 60) = 63.2 kg. The minimum of 63.2 kg and 52.5 kg is 52.5 kg. Apply the Cockcroft-Gault equation for males: CrCl = ((140 - age) * weight) / (72 * serum creatinine) = ((140 - 41) * 52.5) / (72 * 0.86) = (99 * 52.5) / 61.92 = 5197.5 / 61.92 = 83.9 mL/min.",
  "answer": "83.9 mL/min"
}

{
  "step_by_step_thinking": "The patient is a 68-year-old male with admission weight 44.5 kg, height 156 cm, BMI 18.3, and serum creatinine 0.8 mg/dL. Since BMI 18.3 is underweight, the adjusted body weight is set to actual body weight: 44.5 kg. Using the Cockcroft-Gault equation for males: CrCl = ((140 - age) x weight) / (72 x serum creatinine). Substituting values: ((140 - 68) x 44.5) / (72 x 0.8) = (72 x 44.5) / 57.6 = 3204 / 57.6 = 55.625 mL/min. Rounded to one decimal place, the creatinine clearance is 55.6 mL/min.",
  "answer": "55.6 mL/min"
}

{
  "step_by_step_thinking": "Patient is a 71-year-old male with height 155 cm, weight 59 kg, and serum creatinine 1.42 mg/dL at admission. BMI = 59 / (1.55^2) = 24.6 kg/m2, which is in the normal range. For normal BMI, use the minimum of ideal body weight and actual body weight. Ideal body weight for a male = 50 kg + 2.3 kg per inch over 5 ft. Height = 155 cm = 61.02 inches, so IBW = 50 + 2.3*(61.02 - 60) = 52.35 kg. Minimum of IBW and actual weight = 52.35 kg. Cockcroft-Gault equation for males: CrCl = [(140 - age) * weight] / (72 * serum creatinine). CrCl = [(140 - 71) * 52.35] / (72 * 1.42) = (69 * 52.35) / 102.24 = 3612.15 / 102.24 = 35.3 mL/min.",
  "answer": "35.3 mL/min"
}

{
  "step_by_step_thinking": "Patient is a 47-year-old female with height 160 cm, weight 60 kg, and preoperative creatinine 0.90 mg/dL. BMI = 60 / (1.60^2) = 23.4 kg/m2, which is normal. For normal BMI, use the minimum of ideal body weight and actual weight. Ideal body weight for females = 45.5 kg + 2.3 kg per inch over 5 ft. Height 160 cm = 62.99 in, so inches over 60 = 2.99. IBW = 45.5 + 2.3 * 2.99 = 52.38 kg. Minimum of IBW and actual weight = 52.38 kg. Cockcroft-Gault for females: CrCl = ((140 - age) * weight) / (72 * creatinine) * 0.85. CrCl = ((140 - 47) * 52.38) / (72 * 0.90) * 0.85 = (93 * 52.38) / 64.8 * 0.85 = 63.9 mL/min.",
  "answer": "63.9 mL/min"
}

{
  "step_by_step_thinking": "Patient is a 53-year-old male with height 168 cm, weight 59 kg, and admission serum creatinine 1.1 mg/dL. BMI = 59 / (1.68^2) = 20.9 kg/m^2, which is normal. For normal BMI, adjusted body weight is the minimum of ideal body weight and actual weight. Ideal body weight for a male = 50 kg + 2.3 kg per inch over 5 ft. Height 168 cm = 66.14 in, which is 6.14 in over 5 ft, so IBW = 50 + 2.3*6.14 = 64.1 kg. Minimum of IBW (64.1 kg) and actual weight (59 kg) is 59 kg. Cockcroft-Gault for males: CrCl = ((140 - age) * weight) / (72 * SCr) = ((140 - 53) * 59) / (72 * 1.1) = (87 * 59) / 79.2 = 5133 / 79.2 = 64.8 mL/min.",
  "answer": "64.8 mL/min"
}

{
  "step_by_step_thinking": "The patient is a 48-year-old woman with height 5 ft 10 in, weight 97 kg, BMI 31 kg/m2, and serum creatinine 1.0 mg/dL. Because her BMI is in the obese range, use adjusted body weight. Ideal body weight for women = 45.5 kg + 2.3 kg per inch over 5 ft. She is 10 inches over 5 ft, so IBW = 45.5 + 2.3*10 = 68.5 kg. Adjusted body weight = IBW + 0.4*(actual weight - IBW) = 68.5 + 0.4*(97 - 68.5) = 68.5 + 11.4 = 79.9 kg. Cockcroft-Gault equation for women: CrCl = [(140 - age) * weight] / (72 * serum creatinine) * 0.85. CrCl = [(140 - 48) * 79.9] / (72 * 1.0) * 0.85 = (92 * 79.9) / 72 * 0.85 = 7350.8 / 72 * 0.85 = 102.09 * 0.85 = 86.78 mL/min. Rounded to one decimal, this is 86.8 mL/min.",
  "answer": "86.8 mL/min"
}

{
  "step_by_step_thinking": "Identify admission values prior to treatment: age = 81 years, sex = female, height = 153 cm, weight = 40 kg, serum creatinine = 1.27 mg/dL. Calculate BMI: 40 / (1.53^2) = 17.1 kg/m2, which is underweight. Per the instructions, if underweight, use actual body weight, so weight = 40 kg. Apply the Cockcroft-Gault equation for females: CrCl = ((140 - age) x weight x 0.85) / (72 x serum creatinine) = ((140 - 81) x 40 x 0.85) / (72 x 1.27) = (59 x 40 x 0.85) / 91.44 = 2006 / 91.44 = 21.94 mL/min.",
  "answer": "21.94 mL/min"
}

{
  "step_by_step_thinking": "The patient is an 83-year-old male with admission creatinine 1.7 mg/dL, height 172.7 cm, and weight 72.6 kg. First calculate BMI: 72.6 / (1.727^2) = 24.3 kg/m^2, which is in the normal range. For normal BMI, the adjusted body weight is the minimum of ideal body weight and actual body weight. Ideal body weight for a male is 50 kg + 2.3 kg per inch over 5 ft. Height 172.7 cm is 68 inches, so IBW = 50 + 2.3*(68-60) = 68.4 kg. Minimum of 68.4 kg and 72.6 kg is 68.4 kg. Apply Cockcroft-Gault for a male: CrCl = ((140 - age) * weight) / (72 * serum creatinine) = ((140 - 83) * 68.4) / (72 * 1.7) = (57 * 68.4) / 122.4 = 3898.8 / 122.4 = 31.85 mL/min. Rounded to one decimal, this is 31.9 mL/min.",
  "answer": "31.9 mL/min"
}

{
  "step_by_step_thinking": "Patient is a 78-year-old male with height 160 cm, weight 62.8 kg, and serum creatinine 0.93 mg/dL at admission. BMI = 62.8 / (1.6^2) = 24.5 kg/m^2, which is normal. For normal BMI, use the minimum of ideal body weight and actual weight. Ideal body weight for a male = 50 + 2.3 * (height in inches - 60); 160 cm is approximately 63.0 inches, so IBW = 50 + 2.3 * 3.0 = 56.9 kg. Adjusted weight = min(56.9, 62.8) = 56.9 kg. Cockcroft-Gault for males: CrCl = (140 - age) * weight / (72 * creatinine) = (140 - 78) * 56.9 / (72 * 0.93) = 62 * 56.9 / 66.96 = 52.7 mL/min.",
  "answer": "52.7 mL/min"
}

{
  "step_by_step_thinking": "The patient is a 55-year-old male with height 188 cm (74 in), actual weight 109 kg, BMI 31 kg/m2, and serum creatinine 1.5 mg/dL. Because his BMI is in the obese range, use adjusted body weight. Ideal body weight for a male = 50 + 2.3 x (height in inches - 60) = 50 + 2.3 x (74 - 60) = 82.2 kg. Adjusted body weight = IBW + 0.4 x (actual weight - IBW) = 82.2 + 0.4 x (109 - 82.2) = 92.92 kg. Cockcroft-Gault equation for males: CrCl = [(140 - age) x weight] / (72 x serum creatinine) = [(140 - 55) x 92.92] / (72 x 1.5) = 7898.2 / 108 = 73.13 mL/min. Rounded to one decimal place, the creatinine clearance is 73.1 mL/min.",
  "answer": "73.1 mL/min"
}

{
  "step_by_step_thinking": "The patient is a 60-year-old male. At the 2012 admission, prior to treatment, his height was 163.1 cm, weight was 58.5 kg, and serum creatinine was 10.7 mg/dL. BMI = 58.5 / (1.631^2) = approximately 22.0 kg/m2, which is in the normal range. For a normal BMI, the adjusted body weight is the minimum of ideal body weight and actual body weight. Ideal body weight for a male using the Devine equation is 50 kg + 2.3 kg per inch over 5 feet. Height = 163.1 cm = 64.2 inches, so IBW = 50 + 2.3*(64.2 - 60) = approximately 59.7 kg. The minimum of 59.7 kg and 58.5 kg is 58.5 kg. Cockcroft-Gault equation for males: CrCl = ((140 - age) * weight) / (72 * serum creatinine) = ((140 - 60) * 58.5) / (72 * 10.7) = 4680 / 770.4 = 6.07 mL/min. Rounded to one decimal place, this is 6.1 mL/min.",
