{
  "step_by_step_thinking": "Use the first-admission values before treatment: age 22 years, female, serum creatinine 141.4 μmol/L. Convert creatinine to mg/dL: 141.4 / 88.4 = 1.60 mg/dL. Apply the 2021 CKD-EPI creatinine equation, which does not use race: for females, eGFR = 142 × min(Scr/0.7, 1)^-0.241 × max(Scr/0.7, 1)^-1.200 × 0.9938^Age × 1.012. Here Scr/0.7 = 1.60/0.7 = 2.285, so eGFR = 142 × (2.285)^-1.200 × 0.9938^22 × 1.012 ≈ 46.5, which rounds to 46 mL/min/1.73 m².",
  "answer": "46 mL/min/1.73 m²"
}

{
  "step_by_step_thinking": "Use the 2021 CKD-EPI creatinine equation at admission: eGFR = 142 × min(Scr/κ, 1)^α × max(Scr/κ, 1)^-1.200 × 0.9938^Age × 1.012 if female. The patient is a 51-year-old woman with admission creatinine 1.33 mg/dL. For females, κ = 0.7 and α = -0.241. Scr/κ = 1.33/0.7 = 1.9. Since 1.9 > 1, min(1.9,1)=1 and max(1.9,1)=1.9. eGFR = 142 × 1^-0.241 × 1.9^-1.200 × 0.9938^51 × 1.012. Calculating: 1.9^-1.200 ≈ 0.4629, 0.9938^51 ≈ 0.7282. eGFR ≈ 142 × 0.4629 × 0.7282 × 1.012 ≈ 48.4 mL/min/1.73 m², which rounds to 48 mL/min/1.73 m².",
  "answer": "48 mL/min/1.73 m²"
}

{
  "step_by_step_thinking": "Identify the admission values to use: 17-year-old male, serum creatinine 3.7 mg/dL, prior to treatment. Use the 2021 CKD-EPI creatinine equation for males: eGFR = 142 × (Scr/0.9)^-1.200 × 0.9938^Age when Scr > 0.9 mg/dL. Substitute the values: eGFR = 142 × (3.7/0.9)^-1.200 × 0.9938^17. Calculate (3.7/0.9)^-1.200 ≈ 0.1833 and 0.9938^17 ≈ 0.8997. Multiply: 142 × 0.1833 × 0.8997 ≈ 23.4. Therefore, the estimated GFR is approximately 23.4 mL/min/1.73 m², which rounds to 23 mL/min/1.73 m².",
